  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 236 AM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nMonday through Wednesday: The overall upper level pattern remains \nlargely unchanged through Wednesday. Mid-level ridging across the \nsouthwestern United States will remain elongated to the east while \nmultiple rounds of vorticity traverse east across the Dakotas and \nGreat Lakes region. Each day will feature highs in the mid 90s with \ndewpoints in the mid to upper 70s. As such, the Extreme Heat Watch \nis in effect through Wednesday. It should be noted that even though \nIQRs are only a few degrees Monday and Tuesday, confidence rapidly \ndecreases starting Wednesday on the overall synoptic pattern. On \nWednesday, the IQR at STL from the NBM is six degrees while the IQR \nby Friday and Saturday rises towards 15 degrees. Confidence is\nalso lower further north (e.g., Quincy, IL) due to the\npossibility of showers and thunderstorms or convective cloud\ndebris starting Monday (IQRs 5 to 10 degrees). \n\nThursday and Friday: The rapidly falling confidence at the end of \nthe long term is due to the closed low that is currently over the \nJames Bay. This can be seen on the WPC Ensemble Sensitivity Analysis \n(ESA) webpage which shows the variance in the extended coming \nfrom how the closed low opens up and ejects east across Ontario \nand Quebec. As the wave opens up, it is forecast to nudge south \nand east shunting the ridging south. How far south is the main \nquestion, as a multiple rounds of showers and thunderstorms \ncould form along a stationary boundary across the region. \nHowever, where and when the wave ejects is still very uncertain.",
